WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.330
Animators love to slip their own little jokes
into the backgrounds of their movies.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.430
But the folks at Pixar have taken things to
a new level, adding tons of hidden references

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.440
to their own past and future films into each
of their movies.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.760
Here's a look at the best of the best.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.599
Toy Story Stories

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:18.730
Woody makes a rousing speech in front of a
bookshelf in Toy Story.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:22.310
If you examine the titles of the books on
the shelf very closely, you'll spot a few

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:27.529
familiar titles: The Adventures of Andre and
Wally B., Tin Toy, and Knick Knack are all

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:31.720
the titles of short films that Pixar produced
prior to Toy Story.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.270
Don't Overlook That Carpet

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.070
There's a tense scene in Toy Story where Buzz
tries desperately to find an escape route

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.320
out of Sid's house, lest he join Sid’s collection
of misfit toys.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.431
But what makes it extra terrifying to those
with an eagle eye is the fact that Sid's carpet

00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:48.780
is the same design as what's in the Overlook
Hotel from the 1980 thriller The Shining.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.000
The Lion King Is Everywhere

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.190
You'll have to squint to see it, but in A
Bug's Life, if you look very closely during

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.239
the scene of Flik soaking in the ambiance
of Bug City, you'll see just how far the phenomenon

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:02.280
of The Lion King's Broadway adaptation has
traveled.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.320
Mrs. Potato Head's Reading Material

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.079
When Mr. Potato Head makes his appearance
in Toy Story 2, he pretty much steals the

00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:09.079
show.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:10.479
And Mrs. Potato Head agrees.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:13.719
When she realizes that he's found her missing
earring, she immediately throws down the book

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:16.270
she's been reading: a storybook of A Bug's
Life.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.920
A Nemo Preview

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.820
There are Easter eggs, and then there are
next-level Easter eggs.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:25.080
You know the bit in Monsters, Inc. where Boo
hands Sully her stuffed Nemo?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.700
Well, Finding Nemo didn't come out until two
years after Monsters, Inc.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.670
It's all part of Pixar's master plan.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.289
An Incredible Cameo

00:01:33.289 --> 00:01:37.209
Pixar also snuck a sneak peak of a future
film into Finding Nemo itself.

00:01:37.209 --> 00:01:40.819
If you look closely at the bit where the kid
in the dentist's office is scared senseless

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:45.209
by the sounds of Nemo's escape attempt, you'll
see that he's reading a Mr. Incredible comic

00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:49.399
book—a full year and a half before The Incredibles
hit theaters.

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:51.189
Rex Isn't Extinct

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:55.039
Of all the things you'd expect to survive
our planet's self-induced ecological disaster

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:58.560
in Wall*E, you'd wouldn't predict that one
of Andy's toys from Toy Story would be on

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.310
that list of items, let alone the one that's
been extinct for millions of years.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.950
Nonetheless, when we're given a look into
Wall*E's sanctuary and see all the junk contained

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.220
within its walls, there's Rex's toothy grin
smiling at you.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.230
Postcard From The Fredricksens

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.910
As we know from the opening credits of Up,
Carl and Ellie Fredericksen traveled quite

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.140
a bit in their youth.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.190
In the process, they apparently made a few
friendships that we've yet to see explored.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.750
If you take a very close look at the bulletin
board in Andy's room in Toy Story 3, you'll

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.260
see that the Fredericksens sent Andy a postcard.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.960
That's considerate of them.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.570
Monster In The Woodwork

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.450
When Princess Merida takes it upon herself
to stroll through the forest, she finds herself

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.950
at the house of a witch.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.420
Merida watches in awe as a magic broom moves
around the room on its own power.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.540
But what's more fascinating is that its path
takes it—and the viewer—past a log which

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.840
has Sully from Monsters, Inc. carved into
it.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.410
A113 Marks The Spot

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:53.540
You may already be aware of how "A113" has
a tendency to show up in just about every

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.530
film produced by Pixar.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.770
It's shown up on a license plate in Toy Story,
a train in Cars, on a tag attached to Git

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:03.450
the rat's ear in Ratatouille, and in many,
many more places.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.400
So what is A113?

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.480
Why not ask the man himself, Pixar's chief
creative officer John Lasseter.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:17.250
“A113 was the animation, um, classroom at
California Institute of the Arts in the character

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.350
animation program

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.710
Thanks for watching!

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.640
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.440
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Pixar Easter egg you like best…

